  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nAmple cloud coverage depicted on IR associated with the main\nsystem to the southwest across much of Utah. The main PVA\nanomalies expected to skirt south of the CWA giving way to\nmountain snow for Utah and into Colorado. Low clouds continue to\nplague the northeastern portions of the viewing area from the\nnorthern half of the Bighorn Basin and across Johnson to\nnorthern Natrona Counties. Patchy areas of fog expected as well\nas has been observed the past couple of mornings for Monday\nmorning as well, not ruling out the occasional dense fog spots \nas well but not warranting a widespread advisory. Otherwise, the\ndisjointed finger of the PFJ continues around the \naforementioned system depicted on WV with the strongest jet max \nof 120kts pushing into its base well to the south where the \nstrongest divergence is evident aloft. Light snow will push in \nfrom Idaho after daybreak and through the mid afternoon hours. \nHighest elevations of the western mountains looking at minimal \nsnow accumulations of 1 to 2 inches (60-70%) and light \naccumulations for passes to the valleys not expecting impacts to\nbe had for Monday. Cloudy skies and relatively light winds \nexpected before improving overnight into Tuesday. \n\nInduced ridging builds in with increasing convergence aloft for\nTuesday and Wednesday with northwest flow expected through the\nremainder of the work week. Expect temperatures to warm back\nover average for this time of year and dry conditions through\nThanksgiving as the PFJ retreats northward. By late Thursday,\nexpect the jet to push in across northern portions of the state\nto see increasing snow chances for the northern mountains,\nextending further south and west later for Friday and over next\nweekend. The upper level pattern looks to be slower with each\nrun and giving way to more of a meridional flow rather than\nzonal as seen from a couple days ago. Regardless, the tightens\nupper level flow will see quick impacting shortwaves during this\ntime with snowfall to be had across much of the higher terrain\nand points west of the Divide. This pattern is not favorable for\nanything significant at this time, especially to the east but\nsomething to keep an eye on in the coming days. The main low\ncoming out of the GOA extending to the Pacific Northwest is \nslower as well looking at weekend's end to early the following \nweek before passage through the upper Rockies. In the meantime, \nenjoy the holidays with some warmer temperatures and ample \nsunshine making for good travel conditions through Thursday.",
